Declarative homed user records.

Drop <name>.user here, then enable the matching unit:

    systemctl enable --now myosi-homed-user@<name>.service

That creates the user (LUKS home, SELinux defcontext, correct storage flags),
binds it to groups declared by active sysexts, and allocates its
/etc/subuid + /etc/subgid ranges.

homed-user-provision searches this directory first, then the baked
/usr/share/myosi/users/. This one is writable and survives A/B image updates;
/usr is read-only erofs, so records cannot be added there.

The file is the "regular" section of a systemd user record. Storage flags
(--storage, --disk-size, --luks-*) are applied by the helper, not fields here.
See homectl(1) and https://systemd.io/USER_RECORD/.

Example — /etc/myosi/users/alan.user:

    {
        "userName": "alan",
        "uid": 1000,
        "gid": 1000,
        "realName": "Alan Example",
        "homeDirectory": "/home/alan",
        "shell": "/usr/bin/fish",
        "memberOf": ["wheel", "video", "render", "input", "kvm"],
        "preferredLanguage": "en_US.UTF-8",
        "service": "io.systemd.Home",
        "enforcePasswordPolicy": false
    }

The home is created with the bootstrap password `changeme`; rotate it with
`homectl passwd <name>`.

Notes:
  - uid 1000 marks the primary user. If that uid is already taken, the helper
    skips creation and binds groups to the existing owner instead.
  - Creation is one-shot, gated on `homectl inspect`. Editing this file later
    does not update an existing record — use `homectl update`.
  - A record is optional: `homectl create` by hand works too, and enabling the
    unit afterwards still binds groups and subids.
  - Pick the username carefully. systemd 259 has no `homectl rename`.

Only files matching <name>.user are read; this README is ignored.
